-- c_periodic_law_table (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- Modern periodic law, periodic table, s/p/d/f blocks
-- ============================================================
('The modern periodic law states that the properties of elements are a periodic function of their', 'Atomic mass', 'Atomic number', 'Number of neutrons', 'Electron affinity', 'option2', 'Moseley (1913): properties are periodic functions of atomic number ($Z$), not atomic mass (Mendeleev).', 'c_periodic_law_table', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The long form of the periodic table has', '7 periods and 8 groups', '7 periods and 18 groups', '6 periods and 18 groups', '8 periods and 18 groups', 'option2', '7 horizontal periods (rows) and 18 vertical groups (columns).', 'c_periodic_law_table', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Elements in the $s$-block of the periodic table have their valence electrons in', '$p$ orbitals', '$s$ orbitals', '$d$ orbitals', '$f$ orbitals', 'option2', '$s$-block: Groups 1 (alkali metals) and 2 (alkaline earth metals) + He. Valence electrons fill $s$ orbitals.', 'c_periodic_law_table', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The $d$-block elements are also called', 'Representative elements', 'Transition elements', 'Inner transition elements', 'Noble gases', 'option2', '$d$-block: Groups 3-12. Called transition elements because they bridge $s$- and $p$-blocks.', 'c_periodic_law_table', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The $f$-block elements include', 'Halogens and noble gases', 'Lanthanoids and actinoids', 'Alkali metals', 'Chalcogens', 'option2', '$f$-block: lanthanoids (4$f$) and actinoids (5$f$). Placed separately at the bottom of the table.', 'c_periodic_law_table', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Elements in the same group have', 'The same number of electron shells', 'Similar chemical properties due to the same number of valence electrons', 'The same atomic mass', 'The same number of neutrons', 'option2', 'Same group $=$ same valence electron configuration $=$ similar chemistry. E.g., Group 1: all have $ns^1$.', 'c_periodic_law_table', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The element with atomic number $35$ belongs to', 'Period 3, Group 17', 'Period 4, Group 17', 'Period 4, Group 15', 'Period 3, Group 15', 'option2', '$Z = 35$: [Ar] $3d^{10}4s^24p^5$. Period 4 (4th shell), Group 17 (halogens). This is bromine (Br).', 'c_periodic_law_table', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The element with configuration [Ar] $3d^5 4s^1$ belongs to', 'Group 5', 'Group 6', 'Group 7', 'Group 1', 'option2', 'Total valence electrons in $d$ and $s$: $5 + 1 = 6$. Group 6. This is chromium (Cr, $Z = 24$).', 'c_periodic_law_table', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The number of elements in the 3rd period is', '$2$', '$8$', '$18$', '$32$', 'option2', 'Period 3: Na to Ar. $3s$ (2) + $3p$ (6) = 8 elements.', 'c_periodic_law_table', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The maximum number of elements in the 4th period is', '$8$', '$18$', '$32$', '$2$', 'option2', 'Period 4: K to Kr. $4s$ (2) + $3d$ (10) + $4p$ (6) = 18 elements.', 'c_periodic_law_table', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Diagonal relationships in the periodic table exist between', 'Li and Mg, Be and Al, B and Si', 'Na and K, Mg and Ca', 'F and Cl, O and S', 'He and Ne', 'option1', 'Diagonal pairs have similar charge/size ratios: Li-Mg, Be-Al, B-Si. They show similar chemical properties.', 'c_periodic_law_table', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The element with configuration [Kr] $4d^{10}5s^25p^3$ is in', 'Group 13', 'Group 15', 'Group 17', 'Group 5', 'option2', 'Valence: $5s^25p^3$ = 5 electrons in $s+p$. Group 15. This is antimony (Sb, $Z = 51$).', 'c_periodic_law_table', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Mendeleev''s periodic table was based on atomic mass and had limitations including', 'It was too accurate', 'The position of isotopes and the anomalous position of certain element pairs (e.g., Ar-K, Co-Ni)', 'It had too few elements', 'It was based on atomic number', 'option2', 'Ar ($40$) before K ($39$) by mass, but K should come after Ar by properties. Resolved by Moseley''s atomic number.', 'c_periodic_law_table', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The metalloids (semi-metals) in the periodic table are found', 'Along the zig-zag line separating metals and non-metals', 'Only in Group 1', 'Only in the $d$-block', 'In the $f$-block', 'option1', 'Metalloids (B, Si, Ge, As, Sb, Te, Po) have properties intermediate between metals and non-metals.', 'c_periodic_law_table', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The aufbau order of orbital filling ($1s, 2s, 2p, 3s, 3p, 4s, 3d, ...$) explains why', 'The 4th period has 18 elements (including $3d$ transition metals)', 'All periods have 8 elements', 'The $f$-block comes before the $d$-block', 'Noble gases have incomplete shells', 'option1', '$4s$ fills before $3d$ (lower $n+l$). This inserts 10 $d$-block elements into period 4, giving 18 total.', 'c_periodic_law_table', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The element with $Z = 114$ (flerovium) is expected to be in', 'Group 14, Period 7', 'Group 4, Period 7', 'Group 14, Period 6', 'Group 18, Period 7', 'option1', '[Rn] $5f^{14}6d^{10}7s^27p^2$. Period 7, Group 14 (same group as C, Si, Ge, Sn, Pb).', 'c_periodic_law_table', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The first ionization energy generally increases across a period because', 'Electron affinity decreases', 'Atomic size increases', 'Nuclear charge increases while shielding remains roughly constant', 'Electronegativity decreases', 'option3', 'Across a period: $Z_{eff}$ increases, atomic radius decreases, electrons are held more tightly.', 'c_periodic_law_table', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The anomalous position of hydrogen in the periodic table is because', 'It is a noble gas', 'It shows properties of both Group 1 (alkali metals) and Group 17 (halogens)', 'It belongs to the $d$-block', 'It has no electrons', 'option2', 'H: $1s^1$ like alkali metals (loses 1e$^-$), but also gains 1e$^-$ like halogens. Unique element.', 'c_periodic_law_table', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The secondary periodicity observed in Groups 13-16 is due to', 'The noble gas configuration', 'The large size of atoms', 'The high electronegativity', 'The poor shielding effect of $d$ and $f$ electrons', 'option4', 'After filling $3d$ (or $4f$), the poor shielding causes higher $Z_{eff}$, affecting properties of subsequent elements (e.g., Ga, In, Tl show irregular trends).', 'c_periodic_law_table', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The knight''s move relationship in the periodic table is observed between', 'Noble gases', 'Adjacent elements', 'Elements in the same group', 'Elements diagonally separated by one period and two groups (e.g., Zn-Sn, Ga-Sb)', 'option4', 'Knight''s move pairs show similar properties due to similar charge/size ratios after $d$-block contraction.', 'c_periodic_law_table', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The theoretical maximum number of elements in the 8th period (if it existed) would be', '$18$', '$32$', '$50$', '$72$', 'option3', 'Period 8: $8s$ (2) + $5g$ (18) + $6f$ (14) + $7d$ (10) + $8p$ (6) = 50. (Though relativistic effects may alter this.)','c_periodic_law_table', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

