('Atomic radius generally _____ across a period (left to right).', 'Increases', 'Decreases', 'Remains constant', 'First increases then decreases', 'option2', 'Across a period: increasing $Z_{eff}$ pulls electrons closer, decreasing atomic radius.', 'c_periodic_trends', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Atomic radius generally _____ down a group.', 'Decreases', 'Increases', 'Remains constant', 'First decreases then increases', 'option2', 'Down a group: new electron shells are added, increasing the distance from the nucleus.', 'c_periodic_trends', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Ionization energy generally _____ across a period.', 'Decreases', 'Increases', 'Remains constant', 'Oscillates randomly', 'option2', 'Higher $Z_{eff}$ and smaller radius make it harder to remove an electron across a period.', 'c_periodic_trends', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Electronegativity generally _____ across a period.', 'Decreases', 'Increases', 'Remains constant', 'Is zero', 'option2', 'Fluorine (top-right) is the most electronegative. Electronegativity increases across a period and up a group.', 'c_periodic_trends', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The element with the highest first ionization energy in Period 2 is', 'Li', 'Be', 'N', 'Ne', 'option4', 'Noble gases have the highest IE in their period due to completely filled shells. Ne: $2081\;\text{kJ/mol}$.', 'c_periodic_trends', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Electron gain enthalpy (electron affinity) is most negative for', 'Noble gases', 'Halogens', 'Alkali metals', 'Alkaline earth metals', 'option2', 'Halogens need just 1 electron to complete their octet. Cl has the most negative EA ($-349\;\text{kJ/mol}$).', 'c_periodic_trends', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The metallic character of elements _____ down a group.', 'Decreases', 'Increases', 'Remains constant', 'Becomes zero', 'option2', 'Down a group: lower IE, easier to lose electrons, more metallic. E.g., C (non-metal) $\to$ Pb (metal) in Group 14.', 'c_periodic_trends', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The first ionization energy of N ($1402\;\text{kJ/mol}$) is higher than that of O ($1314\;\text{kJ/mol}$) because', 'O has a noble gas configuration', 'O is larger than N', 'N has more protons', 'N has a half-filled $2p^3$ configuration (extra stability)', 'option4', 'Half-filled $2p^3$ in N has extra exchange energy stabilization. Removing an electron disrupts this, requiring more energy.', 'c_periodic_trends', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The first IE of Be ($900\;\text{kJ/mol}$) is higher than that of B ($801\;\text{kJ/mol}$) because', 'Be has a fully filled $2s^2$ subshell (extra stability)', 'B is larger', 'Be has more neutrons', 'B has a noble gas configuration', 'option1', 'Fully filled $2s^2$ in Be is more stable. B loses a $2p$ electron (less tightly held, easier to remove).', 'c_periodic_trends', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The electron gain enthalpy of fluorine is less negative than that of chlorine because', 'Cl has fewer electrons', 'F is larger than Cl', 'F is very small, causing strong electron-electron repulsion in the compact $2p$ shell', 'F has a positive EA', 'option3', 'F: tiny atom, adding an electron to the already crowded $2p$ shell causes significant repulsion. Cl ($3p$) has more space.', 'c_periodic_trends', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ionic radius of Na$^+$ is _____ the atomic radius of Na.', 'Larger than', 'Smaller than', 'Equal to', 'Unrelated to', 'option2', 'Cations are smaller than their parent atoms (lost an electron shell or reduced shielding). Na: $186\;\text{pm}$, Na$^+$: $95\;\text{pm}$.', 'c_periodic_trends', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ionic radius of Cl$^-$ is _____ the atomic radius of Cl.', 'Smaller than', 'Larger than', 'Equal to', 'Zero', 'option2', 'Anions are larger than their parent atoms (extra electron increases repulsion, expanding the electron cloud). Cl: $99\;\text{pm}$, Cl$^-$: $181\;\text{pm}$.', 'c_periodic_trends', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Among Na$^+$, Mg$^{2+}$, Al$^{3+}$, and Si$^{4+}$ (isoelectronic with Ne), the smallest ion is', 'Na$^+$', 'Mg$^{2+}$', 'Al$^{3+}$', 'Si$^{4+}$', 'option4', 'All have 10 electrons. Higher nuclear charge ($Z$) pulls electrons closer. Si$^{4+}$ ($Z = 14$) is smallest.', 'c_periodic_trends', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The second ionization energy of Na is much higher than the first because', 'Na has only one electron', 'The second electron must be removed from a noble gas core ($2p^6$)', 'The second electron is in the $1s$ orbital', 'Na$^+$ is larger than Na', 'option2', 'Na: $[Ne]3s^1$. First IE removes the $3s$ electron. Second IE must break into the stable Ne core — much harder.', 'c_periodic_trends', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The correct order of atomic radii is', 'Li $>$ Na $>$ K', 'K $>$ Na $>$ Li', 'Na $>$ K $>$ Li', 'Li $>$ K $>$ Na', 'option2', 'Down Group 1: K ($227\;\text{pm}$) $>$ Na ($186\;\text{pm}$) $>$ Li ($152\;\text{pm}$).', 'c_periodic_trends', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The correct order of ionization energy is', 'B $>$ Be $>$ C', 'Be $>$ B $>$ C', 'C $>$ Be $>$ B', 'C $>$ B $>$ Be', 'option3', 'IE: C ($1086$) $>$ Be ($900$) $>$ B ($801$). Be $>$ B due to $2s^2$ stability. C $>$ Be due to higher $Z_{eff}$.', 'c_periodic_trends', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The inert pair effect explains why', 'Heavier $p$-block elements prefer oxidation states 2 less than the group valence (e.g., Tl$^+$ over Tl$^{3+}$, Pb$^{2+}$ over Pb$^{4+}$)', 'All elements show the same oxidation state', 'Noble gases are inert', 'Transition metals have variable oxidation states', 'option1', 'The $ns^2$ electrons become reluctant to participate in bonding for heavier elements due to poor shielding by $d/f$ electrons and relativistic effects.', 'c_periodic_trends', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Lanthanoid contraction refers to', 'The contraction of $s$-block elements', 'The expansion of lanthanoids', 'The steady decrease in atomic/ionic radii across the lanthanoid series due to poor shielding by $4f$ electrons', 'The increase in size of actinoids', 'option3', '$4f$ electrons shield poorly. Increasing $Z$ across the series contracts the radii. This affects post-lanthanoid elements (Hf has similar radius to Zr).', 'c_periodic_trends', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The electron gain enthalpy of noble gases is', 'Highly negative', 'Positive (endothermic)', 'Zero', 'Slightly negative', 'option2', 'Noble gases have completely filled shells. Adding an electron requires placing it in a higher energy shell — unfavourable (positive EA).', 'c_periodic_trends', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Among O$^{2-}$, F$^-$, Na$^+$, Mg$^{2+}$ (all isoelectronic with Ne), the correct order of ionic radii is', 'Mg$^{2+} >$ Na$^+ >$ F$^- >$ O$^{2-}$', 'O$^{2-} >$ F$^- >$ Na$^+ >$ Mg$^{2+}$', 'F$^- >$ O$^{2-} >$ Na$^+ >$ Mg$^{2+}$', 'Na$^+ >$ Mg$^{2+} >$ F$^- >$ O$^{2-}$', 'option2', 'All have 10 electrons. Lower $Z$ = less pull on electrons = larger radius. O ($Z=8$) $>$ F ($9$) $>$ Na ($11$) $>$ Mg ($12$).', 'c_periodic_trends', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Allred-Rochow electronegativity scale is based on', 'Electron affinity', 'Ionization energy', 'The electrostatic force exerted by the nucleus on valence electrons', 'Bond dissociation energy', 'option3', 'Allred-Rochow: $\chi = 0.359(Z_{eff}/r^2) + 0.744$. Based on the force experienced by valence electrons.', 'c_periodic_trends', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

